WEBVTT

00:00:00.292 --> 00:00:06.912
At 8:32 PM Universal Time on
November 18, 1929, six transatlantic

00:00:06.942 --> 00:00:10.286
telegraph cables south of Newfoundland
went dead at the exact same

00:00:10.316 --> 00:00:13.529
instant. An undersea
earthquake had just struck.

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:16.620
What baffled engineers was
the next thirteen hours.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.210
Fifty-nine minutes after shaking
stopped, a seventh cable snapped

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.931
farther south. Three
hours in, an eighth.

00:00:22.971 --> 00:00:26.666
At thirteen hours seventeen
minutes, a twelfth tore apart five

00:00:26.696 --> 00:00:28.477
hundred kilometers offshore.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.064
Something unseen had marched across
the pitch-black Atlantic floor

00:00:32.094 --> 00:00:35.477
at highway speed, cutting
steel lines one by one.

00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:39.171
For twenty-three years, nobody
on Earth could prove what it was.

00:00:39.477 --> 00:00:43.403
In 1929, the seabed off
Newfoundland was the busiest cable

00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:46.947
crossroads on Earth— linking
North American stock markets and

00:00:46.977 --> 00:00:49.068
newsrooms directly to Europe.

00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:52.089
Each deep-sea cable was
armored for the abyss:

00:00:52.129 --> 00:00:56.458
a stranded copper core, gutta-percha
latex insulation, tanned jute,

00:00:56.498 --> 00:00:58.420
and galvanized steel wires.

00:00:58.643 --> 00:01:02.620
Automatic recorders stamped the
exact minute any circuit failed.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.882
On shore, a Wheatstone bridge
measured the surviving copper's

00:01:05.912 --> 00:01:09.778
electrical resistance— converting
ohms straight into nautical miles

00:01:09.808 --> 00:01:10.786
to the break.

00:01:11.022 --> 00:01:15.327
When that magnitude 7.2 quake shook
the Grand Banks, it also sent a

00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:19.220
devastating tsunami onto Newfoundland's
Burin Peninsula, claiming

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.597
twenty-eight lives along Placentia Bay.

00:01:21.892 --> 00:01:25.879
At sea, repair ships worked nearly
a year grappling severed lines

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:30.020
from three miles down. When
hydrographer Vladimir de Smitt mapped

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.270
all twenty-eight breaks across
twelve cables, an extraordinary

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.622
pattern emerged.

00:01:34.844 --> 00:01:38.566
Every cable on the shallow
continental shelf stayed intact.

00:01:38.606 --> 00:01:41.828
Six cables on the steep continental
slope around the epicenter

00:01:41.858 --> 00:01:45.748
failed simultaneously within
sixty seconds, crushed by instant

00:01:45.778 --> 00:01:46.691
slumps.

00:01:46.918 --> 00:01:50.796
Then six deeper cables snapped
downslope in strict north-to-south

00:01:50.826 --> 00:01:54.667
order: fourteen minutes, fifty-nine
minutes, three hours three

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:58.842
minutes, nine hours one minute,
ten hours eighteen, and thirteen

00:01:58.872 --> 00:02:02.835
hours seventeen minutes— nearly
five thousand meters deep, where two

00:02:02.865 --> 00:02:05.377
hundred miles of wire
vanished under sand.

00:02:05.681 --> 00:02:09.219
Why would steel cables break half
a day after an earthquake stopped?

00:02:09.249 --> 00:02:14.741
In Nature, geologist J. W. Gregory
blamed parallel faults unzipping

00:02:14.771 --> 00:02:18.954
southward. Yet seismographs
recorded only two aftershocks—

00:02:18.994 --> 00:02:21.207
both back at the original epicenter.

00:02:21.431 --> 00:02:25.527
Others blamed wet silt weighing
cables down, or erosion from the

00:02:25.557 --> 00:02:28.369
overhead tsunami.
Physics ruled both out:

00:02:28.409 --> 00:02:32.374
the tsunami crossed the Atlantic
ten times faster than the breaks.

00:02:32.414 --> 00:02:35.633
For two decades, the
cold case stood unsolved.

00:02:35.936 --> 00:02:38.881
The key came from a glass
flume in the Netherlands.

00:02:38.921 --> 00:02:43.042
Testing Reginald Daly's canyon
hypothesis, Philip Kuenen released

00:02:43.072 --> 00:02:46.901
muddy slurries underwater— proving
that sediment-heavy water plunges

00:02:46.931 --> 00:02:50.373
beneath clear water like
a dense liquid avalanche.

00:02:50.608 --> 00:02:54.500
Still, skeptics doubted a tank
experiment could span an ocean.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:58.867
Until December 1952, when Bruce
Heezen and Maurice Ewing at

00:02:58.897 --> 00:03:03.418
Columbia's Lamont Observatory
reopened those 1929 Western Union

00:03:03.448 --> 00:03:04.302
logs.

00:03:04.524 --> 00:03:07.928
They realized the six delayed
cables were tripwires across an

00:03:07.958 --> 00:03:12.227
abyssal racetrack. Two hundred
cubic kilometers of shaken slope mud

00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:16.181
had mixed with seawater into a
roaring turbidity current hundreds of

00:03:16.211 --> 00:03:17.302
meters tall.

00:03:17.536 --> 00:03:21.438
Plotting cable distance against
outage time turned broken wires into

00:03:21.468 --> 00:03:25.661
a speedometer. Heezen and Ewing
calculated peak speeds near fifty

00:03:25.691 --> 00:03:28.236
knots, slowing to twelve on the plain—

00:03:28.276 --> 00:03:32.384
later refined in 1988 to
sixty-seven kilometers an hour.

00:03:32.678 --> 00:03:35.672
A bold reconstruction still
needed physical proof.

00:03:35.712 --> 00:03:40.245
If the 1929 flow outran the
southernmost cable, a fresh sheet of

00:03:40.275 --> 00:03:44.046
coastal sand had to lie out
on the Sohm Abyssal Plain.

00:03:44.275 --> 00:03:48.043
Lamont ships lowered steel piston
corers more than three miles to

00:03:48.073 --> 00:03:52.085
the bottom. Atop ancient deep-sea
clay sat a sharp graded bed of

00:03:52.115 --> 00:03:56.674
sand, pebbles, and shallow-water
shells— dropped coarse-grains-first

00:03:56.714 --> 00:03:59.104
as the 1929 current slowed.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:03.160
Beside Marie Tharp's pioneering
ocean-floor maps, that core settled

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:06.535
the debate. The abyss
was not a stagnant tomb—

00:04:06.575 --> 00:04:09.725
it was a living landscape
carved by canyons and submarine

00:04:09.755 --> 00:04:10.890
avalanches.

00:04:11.185 --> 00:04:14.203
Today, that discovery
protects the digital world.

00:04:14.243 --> 00:04:18.582
Over 1.8 million kilometers of
garden-hose-thin fiber-optic cables

00:04:18.612 --> 00:04:22.358
cross the seafloor, carrying
more than ninety-nine percent of

00:04:22.398 --> 00:04:24.469
intercontinental internet traffic.

00:04:24.689 --> 00:04:26.931
And the same geohazard still strikes.

00:04:26.971 --> 00:04:31.989
On January 15, 2022, Hunga Tonga's
eruption unleashed undersea

00:04:32.019 --> 00:04:35.644
volcanic currents reaching
122 kilometers an hour—

00:04:35.684 --> 00:04:38.574
the fastest seafloor
flows ever recorded.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.333
Once again, cables clocked the surge:

00:04:41.373 --> 00:04:45.506
Tonga's domestic line snapped at
4:30 AM, and its international

00:04:45.536 --> 00:04:49.887
fiber broke seventy-four minutes
later— wrecking 194 kilometers of

00:04:49.917 --> 00:04:53.103
cable and isolating a
nation for five weeks.

00:04:53.333 --> 00:04:58.400
Armed with the lessons of 1929 and
2022, modern engineers map canyon

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:03.096
chutes with multibeam sonar, detour
around unstable slopes, and bury

00:05:03.126 --> 00:05:04.578
coastal crossings.

00:05:04.852 --> 00:05:08.633
Every packet of light crossing an
ocean today rides on glass threads

00:05:08.663 --> 00:05:12.444
guarded by what twelve broken
telegraph wires accidentally recorded

00:05:12.474 --> 00:05:18.045
on November 18, 1929— over thirteen
hours and seventeen minutes.
